Title:
    _**THE LAST BUS**_
Credit: Written by
Author: A. Writer
Source: Story by A. Writer
Draft date: 25 August 2026
Contact:
    a.writer@example.com
    Birmingham

FADE IN:

INT. BUS SHELTER - NIGHT #1#

Rain hammers the perspex. MAYA, 30s, coat buttoned to the chin, watches the empty road. A timetable flaps loose from its frame.

She checks her phone. No signal.

MAYA
(to herself)
Twenty past. It said twenty past.

A man steps in out of the rain — GEORGE, 60s, soaked through, carrying a cake box tied with string.

GEORGE
You've missed it, love. Last one went at ten.

MAYA
That's not what the app says.

GEORGE
The app's never stood out here in the rain.

He sets the cake box down on the bench between them, very carefully, and looks at it for a long moment before he says anything else. The rain gets heavier. Somewhere behind them a shutter comes down with a bang that makes them both jump, and neither of them mentions it.

GEORGE (CONT'D)
It's her birthday. Was. Is. You never know which one to say, after.

MAYA
I'm sorry.

GEORGE
Don't be. Sit down. It's a big cake.

MAYA ^
I really should—

GEORGE
It's a *very* big cake.

She sits.

EXT. BUS SHELTER - CONTINUOUS

Headlights sweep the wet road. A bus — impossibly, gloriously — pulls in.

MAYA (V.O.)
He was right about the cake.

CUT TO:

.A KITCHEN, YEARS LATER

The same cake box, folded flat, pinned to a corkboard.

> THE END <

FADE OUT.
